 first major issue is the global state in EventBus—handlers persist across test runs because they're stored at the module level rather than per instance. The second bug is that BillingService.handleRefund loses its `this` context.\n\nThe third bug is in calculateOrderTotal, which doesn't round the final result and compounds errors through intermediate rounding. The fourth bug is in OrderRepository.update, which overwrites the existing order instead of merging the updates into it.\n\nFor the update method, the fix is to spread the existing order first, then apply the updates on top. This is critical because cancelOrder relies on the order having its items field intact when it emits the CANCELLED event. Let me trace through what happens when creating an order to understand the full flow. order with all fields including `items`\n2. `await this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CREATED, event)` - ok\n3. `const payment = await this.billingService.chargePayment(...)` - ok\n4. `this.repo.update(id, { status: 'confirmed', total: payment.amount })` - BUG! This replaces the stored order with just `{ status: 'confirmed', total: ..., id, updatedAt }`, losing `items`, `customerId`, etc.\n5. `return this.repo.findById(id)!` - returns the broken order\n\nNow when `cancelOrder` is called:\n1. `const order = this.repo.findById(id)` - returns broken order with no `items`\n2. `order.items.map(...)` - CRASH: \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')\"\n\nThat's exactly the error we see!\n\nThe fix for the update method is to merge the existing order with the updates instead of replacing it entirely. Then there's another bug in the inventory service—the cancel event doesn't include a `status` field, but the handler is checking for one, so it returns early without releasing stock back to inventory.\n\nFor the refund window bug, the condition uses `>=` when it should use `>` to allow refunds on exactly day 30. The test confirms this: when `daysSinceCreation` equals 30, the condition `30 >= 30` is true and throws an error, but it should allow the refund instead.\n\nThe second test case with 31 days would correctly reject with `>`, but it's actually failing due to the EventBus global state issue affecting the inventory reservation, not the refund window logic itself.\n\nLet me trace through all six bugs I've identified: the EventBus handlers accumulating across instances, the missing `.bind(this)` in BillingService, the rounding issue in calculateOrderTotal, the OrderRepository not merging fields on update, the unnecessary status check in InventoryService blocking stock release, and the off-by-one comparison in the refund window. Now I need to fix each one systematically.\n\nActually, let me reconsider bug 5 by looking at the inventory test more carefully—the test event does include a status field, so I need to verify whether that's actually the problem or if something else is blocking the stock release. test DOES include `status: 'cancelled'`, so it SHOULD work. But it's failing with \"Insufficient stock\" at the CREATED event. That means the failure is entirely due to bug #1 (global handlers).\n\nLet me reconsider: are bugs 5 and 6 real bugs?\n\nFor the order service's cancellation flow (test \"releases stock when order is cancelled\"):\n1. `createOrder(...)` → order confirmed, stock reserved (if no global handlers bug)\n2. `cancelOrder(...)` → `cancelOrder` calls `this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED, { orderId, reason, items, timestamp })` (no `status` field)\n3. `handleOrderCancelled` checks `eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled'` → `undefined !== 'cancelled'` → returns early!\n4. Stock NOT released\n\nSo bug 5 IS real - the cancelled event payload never includes a `status` field, causing the inventory handler to bail out early without releasing stock. The fix is straightforward: either add `status: 'cancelled'` to the event payload, or remove the redundant status check entirely since the event type itself already indicates cancellation